      "weight_variable_name": null,
      "weight_fields_interpretation": "The study uses PISA 2022 Spanish student microdata and applies an end-to-end pipeline (pre-processing, dimensionality reduction, clustering, then cluster classification). The manuscript reports using the entire Spanish student sample (n = 30,800) and constructs derived variables from 74 student-context variables, but it does not report any explicit use of PISA sampling weights or replicate weights (e.g., BRR/jackknife) in the analysis section. Because the paper focuses on k-means clustering and supervised classification with SHAP-based interpretability, it is unclear whether complex survey design weights were applied; the safest interpretation is that weights were not explicitly incorporated. Analysts should, when replicating, consider using PISA student sampling weights for population-representative estimates and to better account for the nested sampling design (students within schools), while noting that the paper’s reported predictive metrics may reflect an unweighted ML workflow."
    },
    "plausible_values_handling": "wle",
    "missing_data_handling": "not_reported",
    "handling_not_reported_explanation": "The paper reports variable-level missingness rates (Table B.3) but does not describe the missing-data handling strategy used in the clustering/classification pipeline (e.g., listwise deletion, imputation method, or model-based handling). This is a reporting gap because missingness is non-trivial for several key variables (e.g., SDLEFF, FAMSUPSL, and other WLE-based constructs), and the choice of strategy can materially affect cluster membership and downstream classification performance. Given the absence of a documented approach, the results should be interpreted as conditional on the authors’ unspecified missing-data procedure.",
    "sample_details": {
      "total_students": 30800,
      "countries": [
        {
          "country_code": "ESP",
          "n_students": null
        }
      ],
      "sample_filtering_criteria": "Used the full available PISA 2022 Spanish student sample (15-year-olds) with n = 30,800. The analytic dataset is constructed from 74 student contextual variables spanning socio-economic/cultural, psychological, cognitive/academic, and ICT areas, which are then transformed into 16 derived variables for clustering. The paper reports missing-value rates per variable but does not specify the exact inclusion/exclusion rule for missingness (e.g., complete cases vs. imputation), nor any additional grade/school-type restrictions beyond the standard PISA 15-year-old target population."
    },
